---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PeeringScene from "../components/PeeringScene.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Peering — J5 Code"
  description="Peer J5 on more than one machine so agents on each can message each other, directly or by polling when a device can't be reached."
>
  <div class="peering">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Your agents can work together across all your devices.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Run J5 on more than one machine and peer them. Agents on each machine see the others in
          their address book and message them the way they message the agent next to them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Different machines can do different things.</h2>
        <p class="intro">
          Most people already work across a few machines. Peering lets each agent reach the colleague
          that has what it needs, instead of you copying messages between windows.
        </p>
        <div class="why-grid">
          <div class="tile"><div class="k">Laptop</div><p>Has Xcode and the iOS simulator, so it runs the mobile builds.</p></div>
          <div class="tile"><div class="k">Cloud VM</div><p>Has lots of resources and stays on overnight.</p></div>
          <div class="tile"><div class="k">Home server</div><p>Can access stuff on your home network.</p></div>
        </div>
        <ul class="points">
          <li><b>Agents see where each participant lives,</b> by the device's name, so they can pick the right one.</li>
          <li><b>You tell your agents what each device is for,</b> in your own instructions: "The laptop has Xcode; the home server can reach the NAS."</li>
          <li><b>Agents never manage connections.</b> They message each other directly, and J5 delivers.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="modes">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Two ways to get a message across.</h2>
        <p class="intro">
          When you peer two devices, J5 works out how messages can get between them. Each direction is
          set up on its own, so a pair can send directly one way and poll the other.
        </p>

        <div class="ways">
          <div class="way">
            <svg viewBox="0 -6 340 96" role="img" aria-label="Two agents joined directly" data-motion>
              <path class="water" d="M0 74 Q 40 68 80 74 T 160 74 T 240 74 T 320 74 T 400 74" />
              <line class="tie" x1="96" y1="22" x2="272" y2="22" />
              <g transform="translate(40 18) scale(1.2)"><g class="bob b1"><use class="agent" href="#j5-boat" /></g></g>
              <g transform="translate(240 18) scale(1.2)"><g class="bob b3"><use class="agent" href="#j5-boat" /></g></g>
            </svg>
            <h3>Direct connection</h3>
            <p class="muted">
              One device opens a connection to the other and hands each message straight to the agent.
              It needs the receiving device to be reachable and on.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="way">
            <svg viewBox="0 -6 340 96" role="img" aria-label="An agent with J5's polling barge beside it" data-motion>
              <path class="water" d="M0 74 Q 40 68 80 74 T 160 74 T 240 74 T 320 74 T 400 74" />
              <g transform="translate(70 18) scale(1.2)"><g class="bob b1"><use class="agent" href="#j5-boat" /></g></g>
              <g transform="translate(170 46) scale(1.2)"><g class="bob barge-bob"><use href="#j5-barge" /></g></g>
            </svg>
            <h3>Polling</h3>
            <p class="muted">
              Messages wait on the device that can be reached. J5 on the other device fetches them over a
              connection it opens itself, and hands each to its agent.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="sub">When to use each.</h2>

        <div class="mode">
          <div>
            <div class="tag"><i></i>Direct connection</div>
            <h3>When a device can reach the other.</h3>
            <p class="muted">
              The sending device opens a connection to the receiving one and delivers the message
              straight to the agent. Delivery is immediate, and nothing is stored in between.
            </p>
            <div class="use">
              <b>Choose it for</b> devices that are always on and can reach each other: two cloud VMs, or
              a VM and a home server on Tailscale. If the receiving device is off, the message can't be
              delivered.
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="scene-figure"><PeeringScene variant="direct" /></figure>
        </div>

        <div class="mode">
          <div>
            <div class="tag"><i></i>Polling</div>
            <h3>When a device can't be reached.</h3>
            <p class="muted">
              The reachable device stores the messages, and the device that can't be reached polls for
              them over a connection it opens itself. Its own messages and replies still go directly.
            </p>
            <div class="use">
              <b>Choose it for</b> a laptop behind an office firewall, or any device other machines can't
              connect to. There's nothing to configure on the firewall or proxy: the laptop only makes
              ordinary outgoing HTTPS connections.
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="scene-figure"><PeeringScene variant="firewall" /></figure>
        </div>

        <div class="mode">
          <div>
            <div class="tag"><i></i>Polling</div>
            <h3>When a device comes and goes.</h3>
            <p class="muted">
              A laptop that sleeps, or a desktop app that's closed, can't take messages even when it
              could be reached. With polling, its messages wait on the other device and nothing is lost.
              When it's back, J5 fetches everything that arrived, in the order it was sent.
            </p>
            <div class="use">
              <b>Choose it for</b> the desktop app, or any device that isn't always on. If you're not
              sure, use polling: it works on any network.
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="scene-figure"><PeeringScene variant="asleep" /></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  import { startMotion } from "../lib/motion";

  startMotion({ scenes: Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-motion]")) });
</script>

<style>
  /* Chart paper, as the homepage's fleet section. */
  .peering {
    --bg: var(--canvas);
    --fg: var(--ink);
    --fg-muted: var(--ink-muted);
    --fg-dim: var(--ink-dim);
    --border: var(--canvas-line);
    color-scheme: light;
    background: var(--canvas);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .wrap {
    max-width: 1080px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 32px;
  }

  section {
    padding: 72px 0;
  }

  h1,
  h2,
  h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 46px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 32px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  h2.sub {
    font-size: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }

  p {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 19px;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 18px 0 0;
  }

  .intro {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }

  .muted {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .hero {
    padding: 84px 0 56px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--canvas-line);
  }

  .modes {
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  .why-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 18px;
    margin-top: 28px;
  }

  .tile,
  .way,
  .scene-figure {
    border: 1px solid var(--canvas-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5);
  }

  .tile {
    padding: 20px 22px;
  }

  .tile .k,
  .tag {
    font: 600 11px var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }

  .tile .k {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .tile p:last-child,
  .way p:last-child {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .points {
    margin-top: 26px;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .points li {
    padding-left: 26px;
    position: relative;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .points li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 4px;
    top: 10px;
    width: 9px;
    height: 9px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--msg-ask);
  }

  .points b,
  .use b {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .ways {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 22px;
    margin-bottom: 56px;
  }

  .way {
    padding: 20px 22px 18px;
  }

  .way svg {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 340px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto 14px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .agent {
    --ink: #12202f;
    --hull: #fbf7ee;
    --sail-l: #e5ddc8;
    --sail-r: #fbf7ee;
    --pennant: var(--msg-reply);
    --pennant-fill: var(--pennant);
  }

  .b1 { --bob-dur: 5.2s; --bob-delay: -1s; }
  .b3 { --bob-dur: 5.8s; --bob-delay: -3s; }
  .barge-bob { --bob-dur: 6.4s; --bob-delay: -1.7s; }

  .water {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--sea);
    stroke-opacity: 0.45;
    stroke-width: 1.2;
  }

  .tie {
    stroke: var(--ink-dim);
    stroke-width: 1.4;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  .mode {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 300px 1fr;
    gap: 36px;
    align-items: start;
    padding: 30px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--canvas-line);
  }

  .tag {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .tag i {
    width: 9px;
    height: 9px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--brass);
  }

  .use {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    margin-top: 14px;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brass);
    background: rgba(201, 162, 77, 0.08);
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .scene-figure {
    padding: 14px 16px 10px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    h1 {
      font-size: 36px;
    }

    .why-grid,
    .ways {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .mode {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 18px;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .wrap {
      padding: 0 20px;
    }
  }
</style>
